# Enums and constants

Across the monorepo, avoid scattering magic strings, ints, or other literals that
encode a closed set of meanings. Prefer **`Enum` / `StrEnum` / `IntEnum`** (or a
small typed constant module) so call sites stay readable and mypy-checked.

## Prefer

```python
# BAD
role = "assistant"
if status == 2:
    ...

# GOOD
class ChatRole(StrEnum):
    USER = "user"
    ASSISTANT = "assistant"

class JobStatus(IntEnum):
    PENDING = 1
    RUNNING = 2
    DONE = 3

role = ChatRole.ASSISTANT
if status is JobStatus.RUNNING:
    ...
```

## When to use what

| Shape | Use for |
|-------|---------|
| `enum.StrEnum` | Closed string vocabularies (roles, modes, providers, CLI choices) |
| `enum.IntEnum` / `enum.Enum` | Closed numeric or mixed symbolic sets |
| `Final` / module-level named constants | Single values reused often that are **not** a set of alternatives (e.g. default model id) |
| Dataclass / config fields | Runtime-configurable values from env — not enums |

## Guidelines

- If the same literal appears in more than one place, or is compared/branched on, lift it.
- Name the enum after the concept (`ChatRole`, not `Constants`).
- Member names are `UPPER_SNAKE`; values match the external wire format when needed.
- Pass enum members at call sites; convert at boundaries (JSON, env, Click) with
  `ChatRole(value)` / `.value` as appropriate.
- Prefer `StrEnum` over bare `str` unions when the set is closed and owned by us.

## Allowed exceptions

- True one-off literals in a single expression (`open("r")`, HTTP `200` in a test)
- Framework / vendor APIs that require raw strings or ints by design
- Pytest markers and very local test fixtures
- Doc / user-facing prose strings

When in doubt, make an enum.
